  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - Central idea: Target an analytical optimum via optimized numerical approximation rather than local search improvements.\n   - Rationale: Previous implementation used local optimization with random perturbations, susceptible to local minima. Fundamental solution requires:\n     (a) Global exploration with symmetrized initial conditions\n     (b) Structural optimization of wavelet-like patterns\n     (c) Novel constraint satisfaction mechanism\n   - Bottleneck: Current approach incrementally refines a 270-point parent solution without exploring fundamental function structures.\n   - Primary approach: Multi-stage optimization combining coarse-to-fine resolution with guided functional transformations.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx subject to:\n     1. 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n     2. \u222b\u2080\u00b2h(x)dx = 1\n   - For numerical solution:\n     - Input: N-point grid with spacing dx=2/N\n     h \u2208 \u211d\u207a with sum(h)*dx=1\n     - Objective value: max_k [ (correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * dx ]\n   - Validity: h satisfies all constraints via projection\n   - Representation: Piecewise polynomial interpolation of analytical candidates\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   A. Input handling:\n     - Read parent solution with N=270 when available\n     - Determine grid spacing dx for starting resolution\n\n   B. Construction stages:\n     Stage 1: Symmetrized initial condition\n       - Generate candidate modes:\n         Mode A: Symmetric triangular pulse\n         Mode B: Discrete sine wave harmonics\n         Mode C: Optimized oscillatory function approximation\n       - Combine modes using Chebyshev coefficients\n       - Apply analytical constraints\n\n     Stage 2: Resolution adaptation\n       - Function transform: Change resolution by interpolation/decimation\n       - Maintain consistency via:\n         - Preserve integral: Use normalized basis transformations\n         - Maintain bounds: Apply grid sharpening\n         - Minimize objective change: Use transport plans\n\n     Stage 3: Directed optimization:\n       - Constraint handling: Use projected gradients with active set detection\n       - Objective smoothing:\n         - Use piecewise linear approximation\n         - Apply Legendre-Fenchel transform to bilinear terms\n       - Formulate as:\n         minimize { max_k [ \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx ] } with given constraints\n\n     Stage 4: Final adjustment\n       - Perform exact projection to satisfy all constraints\n       - Validate against original evaluator\n       - Record best candidate and corresponding C5\n\n   C. Detailed operations:\n     - Projection method:\n        design: Find minimum perturbation to satisfy:\n          sum(h)*dx=1\n          0\u2264h\u22641 everywhere\n        use: Gradient projection with constraint violations as basis\n\n     - Resolution change:\n        if N_new != N_old:\n          - Interpolate to new grid\n          - Project to feasible set\n          - Adjust scale to preserve integral\n\n     - Optimization objective:\n        use: Bundle method for non-smooth maxima\n\n   D. Verification:\n        - Implement exact constraint checking\n        - Periodically validate with reference evaluator\n        - Maintain historical best solution\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   A. Mode combinations (used in Stage 1):\n     - 3 primary modes with coordinates in hypercube [0,1] for coefficients\n     - Each mode defined by orthogonal basis functions\n     - Default: Ballpark range of modes: [1,8] oscillatory modes\n\n   B. Resolution adaptation:\n     - Implement two strategies:\n       Adaptive: Heuristic-based grid adjustment\n       Fixed: Use fixed resolution through project\n     - Check at 3/4 budget point\n\n   C. Constraint handling variations:\n     - Projection: Gradient-based\n     - Active-set: Identify binding constraints\n     - Penalty: Use infeasibility measures\n\n   D. Mode initialization:\n     - 3 independent parameterizations:\n        Input normalization\n        Projected gradient method\n        Stochastic rounding\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Time budget: 1300 seconds\n   - Breakdown:\n     - Mode generation: 10%\n     - Optimization sweeps: 70%\n     - Construction variations: 20%\n\n   Robustness checks:\n     - Early termination if improving rate stagnates\n     - Validation after half budget\n     - Fallback to closest feasible solution if no progress\n\n   Construction fallback:\n     - Use mode-constrained projection from parent\n     - Active-set violation adjustment\n     - Successive quadratic approximation\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Final checks:\n       1. h satisfies 0\u2264h\u22641 with tolerance 1e-12\n       2. Integral condition: abs(sum(h)*dx - 1) < 1e-12\n       3. Objective: Check C5 value matches reference implementation\n       4. Fallback: Return parent solution upon timeout\n\n   - Post-optimization refinement:\n       1. Run local optimizer from best candidate\n       2. Check feasibility\n       3. Re-evaluate objective\n\n   - Return format:\n        (h_values, C5_bound, N)\n        with C5 the computed upper bound",
